      "transcriber_notes": [
        "Layout: printed as two side-by-side blocks of 15 lines each, with no column headings; reproduced as four columns (left block's year and figure, then right block's year and figure), row i of the left block beside row i of the right block. The column headings 'A.D.' / 'Population' are supplied; 'A.D.' is printed only on the first line of each block and is kept in that cell. Leader dots omitted.",
        "Reading order as printed here: each block reads down its column (left: 1381 … 1882, then 1412 and 1662 out of order at the foot; right: 1710 … 1923). The ditto marks ',,' repeat the year above them in the same column, so as printed they fall under 1743 (two), 1760 and 1792. The same figures appear in the 1912 and 1921-2 editions set as two blocks that read across each line; there the dittos belong to 1753, 1760 and 1812, and 1412 and 1662 come second and fourth. This edition appears to have re-set that table column by column, which moved 1412 and 1662 to the foot of the left block and changed which years the ditto marks repeat. Transcribed as printed here, not corrected.",
        "Right block, first line: two figures printed for 1710, '23,312,200 & 27,241,129', kept in one cell.",
        "Year '1580': last digit partly broken in the scan (left half of a 0 visible); read as 0. '377,636,000' (1885): first digit slightly broken, reads as 3.",
        "The '* Page 3.' footnote belongs to the asterisk after 342,639,000 in the text above this table, not to the table itself."
      ],
      "context": "On printed p. 2, under the running head \"POPULATION\", the list follows census-based totals (329,542,000, or 331,000,000 with Tibet added) and W. W. Rockhill's 1904 estimate of 270,000,000 for China Proper. The book introduces it as showing \"the estimated population at various dates (obtained from various sources and abstracted chiefly from 'The Middle Kingdom')\" together with \"the latest official estimates of the population\" (the lines for 1895–1923), and names no source for any single figure. As printed here the two blocks read down their columns, 1412 and 1662 stand out of order at the foot of the left block, and the ditto marks (,,) repeat the year above them; the earlier editions set the same figures to be read across each line, where the repeated years are 1753, 1760 and 1812 (see the transcriber's notes). The text that follows turns to the Post Office estimates made since 1919 and the comparison tables on the following pages.",
